WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.795
It is eight in the morning.

00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:07.179
Helen needs a Year Six maths teacher for nine, so she messages Edwin on WhatsApp.

00:00:08.379 --> 00:00:12.476
Ben has already completed background screening in llama dot I D.

00:00:12.476 --> 00:00:16.190
His approval is recorded in Edwina before matching begins.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.719
Sarah receives Helen’s message as a booking request.

00:00:20.719 --> 00:00:24.880
Edwina finds six potential matches for the school’s requirements.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.485
Experience, availability and an on-time arrival narrow the pool.

00:00:30.485 --> 00:00:35.647
Previous school and trust experience, plus feedback, help rank the matches.

00:00:36.847 --> 00:00:43.737
Ben sees the request on his Today page, alongside availability, timesheets, payroll setup and learning.

00:00:43.737 --> 00:00:46.480
The urgent assignment is the main action.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.163
Ben confirms he can arrive by eight forty-five.

00:00:51.163 --> 00:00:53.830
His reply updates Sarah’s shortlist.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.646
Sarah reviews Ben’s fit and expected arrival, then sends him the offer.

00:01:00.846 --> 00:01:05.501
Ben reviews the school, pay, working hours and arrival instructions.

00:01:05.501 --> 00:01:08.307
He acknowledges the briefing and accepts.

00:01:08.307 --> 00:01:10.634
Everyone follows the same booking.

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:17.516
Acceptance creates a placement in Bullhorn, so Sarah does not need to enter the booking again.

00:01:18.716 --> 00:01:23.635
The school sees Ben confirmed, his expected arrival and a link to the booking.

00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:28.049
Helen can focus on welcoming the educator rather than chasing updates.

00:01:29.249 --> 00:01:32.587
Helen messages Ben with reception instructions.

00:01:32.587 --> 00:01:35.428
He receives them against the assignment.

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.984
She can also contact Sarah at Edwin.

00:01:39.184 --> 00:01:46.424
Candidates can also browse recommended jobs, compare dates, pay and journey information, and register interest.

00:01:46.424 --> 00:01:50.990
Their schedule, pay records and learning remain part of the wider app.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:55.877
For another booking, no Edwin candidate is available.

00:01:55.877 --> 00:01:59.912
Edwin sends the requirement to BrightPath, another agency.

00:01:59.912 --> 00:02:03.529
BrightPath proposes an educator for Edwin to review.

00:02:03.529 --> 00:02:05.964
Edwin remains the school’s contact.

00:02:07.164 --> 00:02:14.703
Rachel’s trust dashboard brings together spend against budget, fill rates, time to fill, open cover and school comparisons.

00:02:14.703 --> 00:02:20.404
She can move from an organisation-wide view into the schools and records that need attention.

00:02:21.604 --> 00:02:28.026
Rachel can explore spend by month, role and school, reasons for cover, timesheets and assurance.

00:02:28.026 --> 00:02:32.374
Charts reveal trends; school and date filters define each report.

00:02:33.574 --> 00:02:37.219
Rachel selects spend by job role for the chosen period.

00:02:37.219 --> 00:02:43.649
She can create a PDF to circulate or export the filtered figures into Excel for further analysis.

00:02:44.849 --> 00:02:54.529
This is the report produced by the prototype’s PDF generator: the selected dates, school scope, role totals and definitions travel with the data.

00:02:54.529 --> 00:02:58.268
The Excel export preserves numeric amounts for analysis.

00:02:59.468 --> 00:03:06.199
The term outlook shows when cover demand may rise, with actual observations separated from the forecast scenario.

00:03:06.199 --> 00:03:10.727
Rachel can inspect the weekly values and planning assumptions before acting.

00:03:11.927 --> 00:03:18.211
The monthly cost view combines worked spend, confirmed future cover and an allowance for further demand.

00:03:18.211 --> 00:03:24.194
It shows a central scenario and range, helping the trust discuss likely budget pressure with Edwin.
